    "lawCode": "Add §14-133, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3697           REVISED MEMO 02/26/2019\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the election law, in relation to\ncampaign funds for use at a business owned by a candidate or the candi-\ndate's family\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nProhibit candidates from using campaign funds to purchase goods or\nservices from a business owned by the candidate or a member of his or\nher family.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill adds a new section 14-133 to the Election Law.\nSubsection 1 of this new section prohibits a candidate from expending\ncampaign funds to purchase goods or services from a business in which\nthe candidate and members of the candidate's family collective have an\nownership or beneficial interest of at least thirty-five percent or, in\nthe case of a partnership or professional corporation, direct or indi-\nrect ownership interest of at least five percent.\n\nSubsection two defines the term \"candidate's family\" to mean a person\nwho is within two degrees of consanguinity or affinity to the candidate.\nPeople who are within two degrees of consanguinity to a candidate are\nthe candidate's children, grandchildren, parents, siblings, and grand-\nparents. People who are within two degrees of affinity to a candidate\nare the candidate's spouse and the children, grandchildren, parents,\nsiblings, and grandparents of the candidate's spouse.\n\nSection two of the bill provides that this act shall take effect imme-\ndiately.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nElection Law section 14-130 prohibits candidates from converting\ncampaign funds for personal use that is unrelated to the campaign or\nholding a political office or party position. Payments to a candidate's\nfamily are allowed only when the \"family member is providing bona fide\nservices to the campaign. If a family member provides bona fide services\nto a campaign, any salary payments or other compensation in excess of\nthe fair market value of the services provided shall be considered\npayments for personal use.\" Election Law § 14-130(3)(iv). Unfortunately,\nsection 14-130 does not prohibit a candidate spending campaign funds to\npurchase goods or services from a business that is directly or indirect-\nly owned by the candidate and/or a member of the candidate's family.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhen a person contributes to a candidate for public office, that person\ndoes so because he or she believes in and wants to support the candi-\ndate. While campaign contributors support the use of their campaign\ncontributions to host campaign events or purchase goods or services\nneeded by the campaign, they probably would not support the use when the\nentity hosting the campaign event or providing the goods or services is\nowned by the candidate or the candidate's family.\n\n\nThroughout our lives, many of us have been told that we should live by\nthe Golden Rule. We should treat others the way that we wish to be\ntreated. This legislation will codify the Golden Rule campaign contrib-\nutions. Candidates will no longer be able to funnel campaign funds to\nthemselves or family members by hosting a campaign event at an event\nhall owned or operated by the candidate or a member of the candidate's\nfamily or purchasing good or services from a business owned or operated\nby the candidate or a member of his or her family.  Under the Golden\nRule for campaigns, candidates will treat and use campaign funds in the\nmanner in which they, as campaign contributors, expect their contrib-\nutions to a candidate to be used.\n\nAs public servants and potential public servants, candidates need to\nremember that they serve or will serve their constituents and all New\nYorkers. They promise to use their best judgment to act in the best\ninterest of their constituents and all New Yorkers. Using campaign funds\nin a manner that provides financial gain to the candidate or a member of\nhis or her family is a form of money laundering that only serves to\nbenefit the candidate and his or her family. The rest of the candidate's\nconstituents are not benefitted by such a use of campaign funds. This\nlegislation will ensure that candidates follow the Golden Rule so that\nneither the candidate nor the candidate's family will reap personal\nfinancial gain from the expenditure of campaign funds.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
